## What I built

A conference scheduler: Next.js (App Router, TypeScript) + Drizzle ORM over better-sqlite3, Tailwind UI, Vitest for unit/integration/component tests, Playwright for e2e.

- **Schema** (`db/schema.ts` + migration in `migrations/`): days, rooms, speakers, talks, talk_speakers, slots, placements — with unique constraints on `(room, slot)` and `talk`.
- **Constraint engine** (`lib/scheduling.ts`): the single place enforcing every rule (break slots, length-vs-slot, room capacity, speaker/track conflicts on *overlapping* slots, uniqueness, day-bounds on slot creation, and re-validation on talk/room updates and deletes) — all wrapped in `db.transaction` for atomicity. Both the API routes and the UI call this same module; the UI has no validation logic of its own, it just posts to the API.
- **API routes**: `/api/placements` (place/unplace), `/api/rooms/[id]`, `/api/slots`, `/api/slots/[id]`, `/api/talks/[id]`, `/api/schedule`.
- **UI** (`app/page.tsx` + `components/ScheduleBoard.tsx`): schedule grid (one column per room, breaks spanning all columns, time-ordered), an unplaced-talks list with inline place controls, and inline error display on rejection (no reload).

## How to run it

```
npm install
npm run seed     # already run — data.db is seeded
npm run dev      # serves on $PORT, default 3000
```

Gates run and their final lines:
- `npm run typecheck` → no errors
- `npm run lint` → no errors
- `npm test` → `Test Files 13 passed (13)`, `Tests 19 passed (19)`
- `npm run build` → production build succeeds, all API routes correctly dynamic
- `npm run test:e2e` → waived (see above); confirmed the dev server itself starts and serves correctly before failing at browser launch

